{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And Jacob saw.</b> This is not a seeing of the eye but a seeing of the heart — and the proof of the seeing is <b>behold, I have heard</b>, and he did not say 'behold, I have seen.'" "<b>For there is grain in Egypt.</b> שבר is a term of purchase, as is said (Deuteronomy 2:6): ״אכל תשברו מאתם״. <b>And it is fitting</b> to reflect that Jacob did not mention in his language either אוכל (food) or בר (grain) but rather שבר — 'and Jacob saw that there was שבר in Egypt'; 'behold, I have heard that there is שבר in Egypt.' And the reason he expressed grain in the term שבר is that the term שבר embraces both grain (תבואה) and food (בר), and also embraces destruction and shattering (שוד ושבר), and therefore he expressed the entire matter in the term שבר. <b>And he said</b> further, 'and buy for us from there,' and did not say 'and buy for us food from there' — and all this so that his expression would embrace the matter of the immediate grain, and hint within it the matter of the future exile. And from this he hinted and said, רדו שמה — 'go down there' — and did not say לכו — 'go' — because they would be enslaved there, as inferred from רד\"ו. And he said, 'and buy for us from there,' to hint that he saw for them both שבר and exile from there." "<b>And live, and not die.</b> For even the great servitude they were destined to endure through the multiplication of troubles — he hinted within his words that they would not be consumed and die from within the troubles, but would live through that שבר. As the Sages, of blessed memory, expounded on (Deuteronomy 32:23): ״חצי אכלה חצי כלין והם אינם כלין״ — 'I will consume half, half are consumed, yet they are not consumed.' <b>And furthermore,</b> the Sages, of blessed memory, derived the word שבר in another sense: from the language of שבר ותקוה — hope, as in 'hope (שבר).' And this is what they expounded: 'And Jacob saw' — what is 'and he saw'? He saw through the speculum of holiness that he still had שבר in Egypt — hope. And it was not actual prophecy that would explicitly inform him that it was Joseph." "<b>Why do you look.</b> Why do you grow gaunt from hunger, from the language of (Proverbs 11): ״ומרוה גם הוא יורה״ — thus Rashi explained. And Rabbenu Hananel, of blessed memory, explained 'Why do you look' — why are you not dispersed, why do you all mingle together, in the manner of what is written (II Kings 14:8): ״לכה נתראה פנים״, and it is written (ibid.): ״ויתראו פנים הוא ואמציהו מלך יהודה״. The Ramban, of blessed memory, explained: 'Why do you look at this place' — for you should have set out immediately when you heard that there was grain in Egypt, for they were already in danger if they did not hasten regarding this matter; and this is the sense of 'and live, and not die.'" "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> 'Why do you look' — Jacob said to them: You are mighty, you are handsome — do not enter through one gate and do not stand in one place, lest the evil eye prevail over you."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 42, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:19:53.981349565Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}